 "desc_header": "IN THIS LESSON",
 "lyrics": "A tiger's stripe pattern is unique like a fingerprint and is also visible on its skin; the stripes help it blend in with grasses and trees; tigers are the biggest cats and very adept swimmers with partially webbed toes, and enter water to cool off (WWF Australia, '10 facts about tigers').\nA newborn giant panda is pink, hairless and about the size of a stick of butter, 1/900th the size of its mother (Smithsonian's National Zoo, 'Giant panda'); the black and white markings appear within a few weeks (National Geographic).\nGiant pandas spend 10 to 16 hours a day foraging and eating bamboo (70 to 100 pounds a day), hold the stems with a 'pseudo thumb', and live in mountain forests in China (Smithsonian's National Zoo, 'Giant panda').\nThe black and white pattern of every Holstein cow is unique, like a fingerprint (Holstein Association USA).\nCows are ruminants: they swallow grass, bring it back up as cud and chew it again; the stomach has four chambers (Britannica, 'cud').",
 "script": "Hello, friends! I am Milo the bluebird.\nToday we are looking for patterns!\nStripes are long lines. Like the lines on a stripy sock.\nSpots are round dots. Like the dots on a spotty ball.\nSome animal friends wear stripes. Some wear spots.\nPut on your looking eyes. Are you ready? Let's go!\nHere comes an orange friend.\nStripes, or spots?\nStripes! Orange and black stripes. It is the tiger!\nAre the stripes only on the fur?\nNo! Here is the fun fact. A tiger's skin is stripy too!\nStripes on the fur, and the very same stripes on the skin underneath.\nAnd no two tigers have the same stripes.\nBut why does a tiger wear stripes?\nThe stripes help the tiger hide in the tall grass. Can you still see it?\nAnd guess what? Most cats do not like water. But tigers are great swimmers!\nSplash, splash! On a hot day, a tiger cools off in the water.\nIt even has a little skin between its toes, like a duck, to help it paddle!\nThe tiger is the biggest cat in the whole world.\nA baby tiger is called a cub.\nSay it with me. Tiger skin is stripy too!\nCan you creep like a tiger? Creep, creep, creep. Pounce!\nQuick check! Is a tiger's skin stripy too? Yes, or no?\nYes! Stripes on the fur, and stripes on the skin!\nHigh five! You have great looking eyes.\nLook at this round friend, with black and white patches. It is the panda!\nNow, what color is a newborn baby panda? Black and white? Or pink?\nPink! Here is the fun fact. A baby panda is born pink!\nLook how tiny! A newborn panda is as small as a stick of butter.\nThe black and white patches grow later, when the baby is a few weeks old.\nA grown-up panda is big. But its baby is one of the tiniest babies of all!\nThe mama panda holds her tiny baby very, very gently.\nAnd what does a grown-up panda eat, all day long?\nBamboo! A panda munches bamboo for most of the day.\nIt even has a special thumb, just for holding the bamboo!\nA panda eats a great big pile of bamboo, every single day!\nCan you say bamboo?\nPandas live far away, in the cool mountain forests of China.\nSay it with me. Baby pandas are born pink!\nCan you munch like a panda? Munch, munch, munch!\nQuick check! What color is a newborn panda? Pink? Or green?\nPink! You remembered!\nWhat a super memory!\nMoo! Look at this farm friend.\nStripes, or spots?\nSpots! Big black spots. It is the cow!\nDo any two cows have the same spots?\nNo! Here is the fun fact. Every spotted cow has her very own spots.\nHer spots are like your fingerprint. Nobody else has the same one!\nLet's count some spots. One spot, two spots, three spots!\nAnd what does a cow eat all day?\nGrass! She munches the grass. Then she chews it all over again!\nA cow has a very big tummy, with four rooms inside, to help her eat all that grass!\nCount the rooms. One, two, three, four!\nAnd where does milk come from? From cows!\nThe milk in your cup, and the cheese on your pizza, both come from cow's milk.\nA baby cow is called a calf.\nSay it with me. Every cow has her own spots!\nCan you chew like a cow? Chew, chew, chew!\nQuick check! Do two cows ever have the same spots? Yes, or no?\nNo! Every cow has her very own spots.\nWave bye-bye to the cow. Bye-bye, cow!\nWow! We found so many patterns. Now it is quiz time!\nGet ready. Think hard!\nWhose skin is stripy too?\nThe tiger! Yes!\nWho is born pink, and as small as a stick of butter?\nThe panda! That's right!\nWhose spots are like a fingerprint?\nThe cow! You remembered!\nYou learned so many new things today.\nStripes on the tiger. Patches on the panda. And spots on the cow!\nCan you find stripes or spots at home today? Maybe on a sock. Maybe on a ball!\nThank you for looking with me. Bye-bye, friends! See you next time!",
